#4471 was merged on subject lines and marked known with one fix queued. It holds 412 unique
reports in 427 exported rows, and four problems rather than one. This page splits them. Each
one has a ticket in
[mega-what-it-shows](https://github.com/t-works/mega-what-it-shows/issues).

<IncidentSplit ticket={4471} />

## What was merged

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## The four tickets

1. **33 · a second cause, booking-v3's date hint.** Day-first tenants who book through
   booking-v3 type the date month first, because the form tells them to. The parser fix does
   not touch them and they have no ticket of their own.
   [#1](https://github.com/t-works/mega-what-it-shows/issues/1)
2. **5 · one-day moves, not month flips.** The desk's own note reads these as a one-day move.
   They stay counted under the parser cause until someone decides where they belong.
   [#2](https://github.com/t-works/mega-what-it-shows/issues/2)
3. **38 · the intake tag names the wrong cause.** 23 parser reports are tagged `hint`, 15
   `hint` reports are tagged `parser`. Intake guesses from the browser; the cause depends on
   the tenant.
   [#3](https://github.com/t-works/mega-what-it-shows/issues/3)
4. **15 · duplicate rows in the export.** 427 rows for 412 reports. A count taken from the
   file is 15 too high.
   [#4](https://github.com/t-works/mega-what-it-shows/issues/4)

<Callout kind="warn">
The 412 is right and the 427 is not. Take the count from this page or from `uniqueById()`,
never from the rows.
</Callout>

## What stays in #4471

The parser cause keeps 374 reports after the 5 one-day moves are set aside. The queued fix
closes those, and nothing else on this page.

## Lessons

- A subject line is not a cause. Merging on one put a second bug inside a known ticket, and
  the ticket still says one issue.
- A guessed field becomes a fact the moment a page prints it. The tag column has been wrong on
  38 reports.
- Count reports once: by id, not by row.

